{"title":"A Workflow for Identifying Viable Crystal Structures with Partially Occupied Sites Applied to Solid Electrolyte: Cubic Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12","authors":"Julian Holland; Tom Demeyere; Arihant Bhandari; Felix Hanke; Victor Milman; Chris-Kriton Skylaris","field_subfield":"Computational Materials Science / Solid-State Ionics","type_of_paper":"Methodology + Applied Case Study (preprint)","executive_summary":"Solid electrolytes are central to next-generation Li-ion batteries because they promise higher energy density, improved safety, longer lifetimes, and reduced production costs relative to liquid electrolytes. Among candidates, cubic Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12 (c-LLZO) stands out for its excellent interfacial stability against metallic Li, but it is not stable at room temperature and is typically stabilised with substitutional dopants on the 24d sites. c-LLZO crystallises in the Ia 3d space group with Li atoms partially occupying the 24d and 96h sites, and there is no consensus on the correct Li occupancy ratio. Prior computational studies have relied on a single experimental occupancy as a starting point to build an idealised unit cell, which raises the concern that predicted structures may not be near the ground state and that derived thermodynamic and observable properties may be poorly described. This study introduces a combinatorial workflow that generates all possible Li site occupancies for a c-LLZO unit cell and orders them energetically. The authors first eliminate structures with overlapping Li atoms by imposing a minimal Li-Li distance of 1.7 Å, then remove symmetry-equivalent duplicates using local sensitive hashing (LSH) to reduce the configuration space from ~2.3 × 10 8 structures to ~2 × 10 6 symmetry-unique structures. They then fit a multiple linear regression (MLR) model trained on 1235 single-point DFT calculations performed with the ONETEP code (PBE functional, 830 eV kinetic energy cutoff) to energetically rank the remaining structures. The workflow finds that all structures occur within 5.8 eV of the lowest-energy structure, that 19 of the 20 lowest-energy structures have a 24d:96h ratio of 8:48, and that four structures within 0.026 eV (1 kT at 298 K) of the ground state share the same 24d configuration with only slight variations in the 96h configuration. The authors argue that a large proportion of previously reported experimental and theoretical structures may not be physically feasible or stable, and they provide all generated structures in a freely accessible database to improve accuracy and reproducibility. The paper is a preprint (ChemRxiv, CC BY-NC-ND 4.0) and does not report a formal bibliography, so top_influential_citations is empty. Notable omissions include the absence of author affiliations, participant demographics (not applicable to a computational study), and detailed hyperparameter reporting beyond the DFT settings.","research_context":[{"The motivation is that solid electrolytes offer higher energy density, improved safety, longer lifetimes, and reduced production costs versus liquid electrolytes.":"higher energy density, improved safety, longer lifetimes, and reduced production costs"},{"c-LLZO has the best interfacial stability of all popular fast ion conducting solid electrolytes against metallic Li.":"best interfacial stability of all popular fast ion conducting solid electrolytes against metallic Li"},{"c-LLZO has two primary polymorphs, the highly conducting disordered cubic LLZO (c-LLZO) and ordered tetragonal LLZO (t-LLZO).":"highly conducting disordered cubic LLZO (c-LLZO) and ordered tetragonal LLZO (t-LLZO)"}]
,"research_question_and_hypothesis":[{"The central problem is that experimental and theoretical works have been unable to uncover the ground state configuration of c-LLZO.":"unable to uncover the ground state configuration of prominent solid electrolyte candidate cubic Li 7 La 3 Zr 2 O 12 (c-LLZO)"}]
